{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The joint probability of $X_1, ..., X_n$ and $\\lambda$ is proportional to $\\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^{X_i}}{X_i!} \\cdot \\frac{\\beta^\\alpha}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)} \\lambda^{\\alpha-1} e^{-\\beta \\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:12:08\u201303:12:43] - The speaker states: 'We have P, X1, Xn. the condition on lambda and p lambda and integral of 0 to infinity on lambda force. So it's just a change rule for our joint distribution.' Then later: 'We just use three, the lambda is the same denominator. We have P, X1, Xn.' This matches the product of Poisson likelihood and prior density."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution of $\\lambda$ given $X_1, ..., X_n$ is proportional to $\\lambda^{\\alpha + \\sum X_i - 1} e^{-(\\beta + n)\\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:17:47\u201303:18:19] - The speaker says: 'we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' This directly states the form of the posterior kernel."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is a Gamma distribution with updated shape $\\alpha^* = \\alpha + \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$ and updated rate $\\beta^* = \\beta + n$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:18:19\u201303:18:48] - The speaker states: 'we can say like, oh, it's very similar to gamma distribution, which is some b and lambda a n minus one, where a n is this one, b n is this one.' Then: 'b and equals to b plus n' and 'a minus one here is a plus xi minus one.' This confirms the updated parameters."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The derivation uses the 'Bau's method' (likely a typo for 'Bayes' method) to avoid full computation by focusing on the form of the posterior distribution",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:19:51\u201303:20:27] - The speaker says: 'and compute everything that depends on lambda. Then find the distribution that has similar lambda theme and just add the constant and say that the distribution is the same. And we can solve it strictly. It's just like method how to avoid too much computation.' This describes the method as avoiding full computation by focusing on the form."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is normalized by a constant such that the integral over $\\lambda$ equals 1",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:19:18\u201303:19:51] - The speaker states: 'because integral of the x is one always. So we can compute that the denominator integral of this is exactly the inverse constant.' This confirms the normalization step."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Gamma distribution prior has the form $\\text{Gamma}(\\alpha, \\beta) = \\frac{\\beta^\\alpha}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)} \\lambda^{\\alpha-1} e^{-\\beta \\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:17:17\u201303:17:47] - The speaker describes the prior: 'It is of the A divided by gamma function on A, and A minus one, exponent minus B lambda.' This matches the Gamma distribution form."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Poisson distribution has the form $P(X = x | \\lambda) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^x}{x!}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:12:43\u201303:13:28] - The speaker states: 'First, I think we already calculated this above. It's the xi and minus lambda n multiplied on lambda xi divided by product of Xi factorial, yeah?' This matches the Poisson PMF."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is conjugate to the Poisson likelihood with a Gamma prior",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [03:18:48\u201303:19:18] - The speaker states: 'we can solve it strictly. It's just like method how to avoid too much computation.' and 'the posterior is a Gamma distribution' which is conjugate to Poisson."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The speaker mentions 'Bau's method' which is likely a typo for 'Bayes' method. The derivation is clear but has some speech errors and unclear phrasing that make it slightly difficult to follow without context."
}
